# Xcode toolchain pin — Primae
#
# ROADMAP F11 (the iOS 27 SDK move). ONE place, read by everything.
#
#   major: 27
#     The estate decision (homelab-architecture #624, 2026-08-18,
#     slug xcode-27-toolchain-decision-2026-08-18) pins Xcode 27 by MAJOR
#     version across every Apple project, with the pin living in each repo
#     and the workstation authoritative, CI following. This file is Primae's
#     half of that decision.
#
#     MAJOR-only is deliberate and it has a cost worth stating: it cannot
#     distinguish 27.0 from 27.2. Both are installed on this workstation
#     (/Applications/Xcode.app = 27.0 release 27A266a,
#      /Applications/Xcode-beta.app = 27.2 beta 27B5019j). A MAJOR pin says
#     both are acceptable; it does NOT say which one built any given artefact.
#     For that, the EXACT build is still recorded with the artefact —
#     `scripts/build_study.sh` prints `xcodebuild -version` on every run, and
#     CLAUDE.md's standing rule requires capturing it. Pin = what we accept;
#     build stamp = what actually ran. Both, not either.
#
#   runner_label: xcode-27
#     What `.github/workflows/ios-build.yml` uses for `runs-on:`. This is the
#     CI half of the pin, and it is a LABEL, not a path.
#
#     History worth not re-deriving: the image ran on macOS 26 until
#     2026-09-10, when GitHub moved it to macOS 27. Before that the
#     `macos-26` + `sudo xcode-select /Applications/Xcode_26.4.app` pair was
#     a second, independent way to name the toolchain — which is exactly why
#     they could disagree. There is now ONE name for it, here.
#
#     F11 recorded a related lesson: a gate that searches for the WRONG
#     identifier returns "absent" indistinguishably from "does not exist" —
#     the 2026-07-07 audit looked for a `macos-27` label, found none, and
#     recorded a CI-availability blocker that had been false since
#     2026-07-16. The image is `xcode-27`.
#
#   deployment_target: 27.0
#     Package.swift `.iOS(.v27)` and all 12 IPHONEOS_DEPLOYMENT_TARGET
#     entries in Primae.xcodeproj. This is a DEVICE-SUPPORT decision, not a
#     toolchain one, and it drops support for every iOS 26 device. The study
#     iPad runs iOS 27.2 (measured 2026-10-01), so the pilot is unaffected.
#
#     Corollary to respect: `.iOS(.v27)` requires
#     `// swift-tools-version: 6.4`. At 6.3 the manifest is rejected outright
#     ("'v27' is unavailable ... introduced in PackageDescription 6.4"), so
#     the tools version is part of this pin, not an incidental detail.
#
# WHY THIS IS NOT THE WHOLE STORY. `xcode-27` is currently a PUBLIC PREVIEW
# image. ROADMAP F11's other gate — swiftlang/swift#88173, an inliner crash
# with exactly our configuration (-O plus -default-isolation MainActor) — was
# cleared for the pilot configuration on 2026-10-01: a Release-Study -O build
# on Xcode 27.2 SUCCEEDED. One clean -O build is meaningful here precisely
# because that bug is an -O-only inliner crash, but it is one compiler run, not
# a cleared gate. If a future -O build fails with a compiler crash, this pin is
# the first thing to revisit.

major: 27
runner_label: xcode-27
deployment_target: 27.0